  "response": "# PR review: `02-react-dashboard`\n\n## Verdict: nothing blocks. 4 warn, 8 ignore.\n\n**Decision rule I applied** (stated up front so you can disagree with the rule rather than the 99 calls):\n\n| Category | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| **Block merge** | `severity: high`, **or** `agent_risk ≥ 0.50` on a `medium`+ finding — i.e. a thing an agent will silently get wrong later |\n| **Warn** | Real defect, cheap fix, but `agent_risk < 0.50` — file it, don't gate on it |\n| **Ignore** | Detector is correct about the code and wrong about the context |\n\n**Nothing in this report clears the block bar.** The scan reports `high: 0`. Peak severity is `0.55`, peak `agent_risk` is `0.45` (`sync_io_in_hotpath`). No detector fired in a correctness or authority class. A merge gate keyed to severity+agent_risk catches zero findings here, and I don't think that's a mis-calibrated gate — I think it's the right answer for this diff.\n\n## The context fact that reframes ~60% of the report\n\nThis is `vercel/next-learn` — a **teaching repository**. I verified it three ways:\n\n- `README.md` — \"starter templates and final code for Learn Next.js courses\"\n- `dashboard/starter-example/app/ui/dashboard/latest-invoices.tsx:17` — `{/* NOTE: Uncomment this code in Chapter 7 */}`\n- Every `exact_duplicate_block` pair is `starter-example` ↔ `final-example`, or `basics/*-starter` ↔ `basics/*-final`\n\nThe duplication **is the product**. The commented-out code **is the exercise**. Deduplicating either would destroy the repo's purpose. That single fact accounts for 46 duplicate-block findings, 8 of 10 commented-code findings, and the one duplicate-component-shape finding — **55 of 99**.\n\n---\n\n# By detector type\n\n| Detector | n | severity | agent_risk | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `exact_duplicate_block` | 46 | 0.55 med | 0.28–0.31 | **Ignore** |\n| `raster_should_be_vector` | 12 | 0.35 low | 0.05 | **Ignore** |\n| `commented_out_code` | 10 | 0.25–0.45 | 0.37–0.39 | **8 ignore / 2 warn** |\n| `design_token_escape` | 8 | 0.40 low | 0.07 | **Ignore** |\n| `sync_io_in_hotpath` | 6 | 0.40 low | 0.37–0.45 | **Ignore** |\n| `oversized_raster` | 6 | 0.40 low | 0.04 | **Warn** |\n| `direct_date` | 3 | 0.35 low | 0.28–0.30 | **1 warn / 2 ignore** |\n| `duplicated_role_status_plan_check` | 2 | 0.55 med | 0.36 | **Warn** |\n| `singular_plural_type_mismatch` | 2 | 0.30 low | 0.28 | **Ignore** |\n| `deep_import` | 2 | 0.35 low | 0.07 | **Ignore** |\n| `magic_domain_literal_scatter` | 1 | 0.50 med | 0.41 | **Warn** |\n| `duplicate_component_shape` | 1 | 0.55 med | 0.28 | **Ignore** |\n\n---\n\n## Warn (4 clusters)\n\n### `magic_domain_literal_scatter` — 1 finding · sev 0.50 med · agent_risk **0.41**\n`crime_00006` — `\"status\"` across 5 files.\n\nHighest agent_risk of anything I'm not ignoring, and the detector found something real. The literal is a **form-field name contract**: `name=\"status\"` in `edit-form.tsx:105,121` must match `formData.get('status')` in `actions.ts:43`. Nothing type-checks that seam — rename one side and the Zod parse silently yields `undefined`.\n\nWarn not block because the Zod `FormSchema` already is a de-facto source of truth, and hoisting HTML `name` attributes into constants is non-idiomatic React. `agent_risk 0.41` is the detector correctly noticing an untyped cross-file coupling; it just isn't worth a merge gate.\n\n### `duplicated_role_status_plan_check` — 2 findings · sev 0.55 med · agent_risk **0.36**\n`crime_00018` (`\"paid\"`), `crime_00019` (`\"pending\"`) — 4 files, 2 expression shapes.\n\nBoth fire on the same pattern: `status.tsx:10,11,15,21` switches presentation on the enum; `edit-form.tsx:108,124` sets `defaultChecked` from it. The \"2 distinct expression shapes\" is `status === \"paid\"` vs `invoice.status === \"paid\"` — same check, different receiver.\n\nHalf the file count is the starter/final split, so the real spread is 2 files per app, not 4. And these are **rendering switches on an enum that is already centralised** (`z.enum` in `actions.ts`, `InvoiceStatus` in `definitions.ts`) — not duplicated policy. Warn: if a third status ever lands, `status.tsx` fails open (renders an empty badge) with no compile error. A `satisfies Record<InvoiceStatus, …>` map would close it.\n\n### `direct_date` — 1 of 3 · sev 0.35 low · agent_risk 0.28\n`crime_00069` — `actions.ts:57`, `new Date().toISOString().split('T')[0]`.\n\nThe detector's own evidence says \"none decide a branch — all record or render the reading,\" which is true but undersells this one: it's the only one of the three that **persists** to the database (`INSERT INTO invoices (…, date)`). That makes `createInvoice` untestable without freezing the clock, and it silently uses server-local UTC date. Worth an injected clock; not worth blocking.\n\nIgnoring `crime_00028`/`crime_00029` (`utils.ts:14`) — that's `formatDateToLocal`, a pure formatter taking the date as a parameter. The `new Date()` there is a parse, not a clock read. Reasonable detector miss.\n\n### `oversized_raster` — 6 findings · sev 0.40 low · agent_risk **0.04**\n`hero-desktop.png` 486.6 KB (×2), `large-image.jpg` 422.4 KB (×2), `opengraph-image.png` 237.5 KB (×2).\n\nGenuinely real page weight, and `agent_risk 0.04` is the detector correctly saying *this will not confuse anyone, it will just be slow*. Halved by the starter/final duplication, so it's 3 distinct assets.\n\nOne caveat worth knowing before anyone \"fixes\" it: `seo/public/large-image.jpg` is almost certainly **deliberately oversized** — it's the subject of the SEO course's image-optimisation lesson. Shrinking it would break the chapter.\n\n---\n\n## Ignore (8 clusters)\n\n### `exact_duplicate_block` — 46 findings · sev 0.55 med · agent_risk 0.28–0.31 · confidence 0.95\n**46% of the entire report, all one structural fact.** Every pair is starter↔final or basics-variant↔basics-variant. The detector is right at 0.95 confidence and non-actionable at 100%.\n\nThis is the report's real problem — not a wrong finding, a **swamped one**. The 4 warns above are buried under 46 correct-but-inert mediums.\n\n### `raster_should_be_vector` — 12 findings · sev 0.35 · agent_risk **0.05**\nAll 12 are `public/customers/*.png` — 56–60px **photographic avatars** (`amy-burns`, `lee-robinson`, `evil-rabbit`…). Photographs cannot be SVGs. This is a detector class limitation: it thresholds on dimensions and can't distinguish an icon from a thumbnail portrait. 12 findings, 6 distinct images.\n\n### `commented_out_code` — 8 of 10 · sev 0.25–0.45 · agent_risk 0.37–0.39\nThe 8 in `starter-example` (`crime_00007`–`00012`, `00014`, `00017`) are course scaffolding, verified verbatim at `latest-invoices.tsx:17`. The detector's stated worry — \"can mislead humans and agents into copying or reviving stale behaviour\" — is *inverted here*: reviving it is the assignment.\n\n**Except two, which I'd warn on:** `crime_00013` (`final-example/app/lib/data.ts:19-20`) and `crime_00015` (`final-example/app/query/route.ts:17-20`) are in the *finished* code with no chapter marker. `data.ts` is likely the artificial-delay demo block, but unmarked commented code in a reference implementation is exactly what agents copy forward. One explanatory line each resolves it.\n\n### `design_token_escape` — 8 findings · sev 0.40 · agent_risk **0.07**\nThese are **Tailwind arbitrary-value utilities** — `px-[14px]` at `edit-form.tsx:100`, `py-[9px]`, `h-[38px]`. Idiomatic Tailwind, not a design-system escape. 8 findings for ~4 distinct values, doubled by starter/final. `agent_risk 0.07` is the detector telling you itself.\n\n### `sync_io_in_hotpath` — 6 findings · sev 0.40 · agent_risk **0.45** ← highest in the report\nThe one I most expected to keep, and the one context most clearly kills. I traced every call site:\n\n```\nbasics/api-routes-starter/pages/index.js:39      export async function getStaticProps()\nbasics/api-routes-starter/pages/posts/[id].js:24 export async function getStaticPaths()\n```\n\nAll 11 call sites across all 6 packages are inside `getStaticProps` / `getStaticPaths` — **build-time SSG loaders that never serve a request**. There is no hot path and no event loop to block. `fs.readdirSync` in a static-site generator is correct code.\n\nWorth flagging back to the product: `0.25.0` already taught this detector to stop charging one-shot scripts (`834d10f`). Build-time framework data loaders are the adjacent case it doesn't yet cover, and this fixture shows it costing the report its top-ranked cluster.\n\n### `singular_plural_type_mismatch` — 2 findings · sev 0.30 · agent_risk 0.28\n`revenue: Revenue[]`. \"Revenue\" is a **mass noun** — it has no distinct plural, so the name can't agree with the array shape. The finding's own evidence flags it as a v1 detector. Textbook `allowedNames` entry.\n\n### `deep_import` — 2 findings · sev 0.35 · agent_risk **0.07**\n`react-syntax-highlighter/dist/cjs/styles/prism` — this is that package's **documented** way to import styles; there is no shallower public entry. The detector's advice (\"file an upstream issue rather than coding around it\") is right in general and inapplicable here.\n\n### `duplicate_component_shape` — 1 finding · sev 0.55 med · agent_risk 0.28\n4 files = 2 apps × (`create-form`, `edit-form`). Strip the starter/final split and it's one real create/edit pair sharing a form-actions `<div>`. Marginal extraction, not a merge concern.\n\n---\n\n## What I'd actually do\n\n1. **Fix now, in this PR** (~10 minutes, all `effort: quick`): add a rationale comment to the two unmarked commented blocks in `final-example` — `crime_00013`, `crime_00015`.\n2. **File as follow-ups**: injected clock in `actions.ts:57`; `satisfies Record<InvoiceStatus, …>` in `status.tsx`; re-encode `hero-desktop.png` and `opengraph-image.png` to WebP.\n3. **Configure, don't fix** — the 55 findings driven by repo shape need a config answer, not a code one. Excluding `**/*-starter/**` and `basics/demo/**` from cross-file duplication detectors would cut the report from 99 to ~44 and surface the 4 real warns at the top. Plus `allowedNames: [\"revenue\"]` and an allowlist entry for `react-syntax-highlighter`.\n\n**One caution on the framing.** The scenario premise — \"a PR landed this on top of an empty baseline\" — means every finding is technically \"introduced by this PR.\" That's a common way to make a review look alarming. 99 findings against a 183-file initial import is a *low* density, and a first-commit diff has no regression to protect. I'd hold this same bar on the next PR, where a new `sync_io_in_hotpath` in a request handler genuinely would block.",
